{"name":"Pickled Bean","hex":"#6e4826","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pickled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of pickle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Preserved by pickling.","Drunk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pickled"},{"term":"bean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of several genera of the taxonomic family Fabaceae that produces large edible seeds or edible seedpods.","The large edible seed of such a plant (for example, a broad bean, navy bean, or garbanzo bean)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hit with a projectile, especially a deliberately aimed blow to the head."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bean"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pickling","blocks":[{"text":"Pickling is the process of preserving or extending the shelf life of food by either anaerobic fermentation in brine or immersion in vinegar. The pickling procedure typically affects the food's texture and flavor. The resulting food is called a pickle, or, if named, the name is prefaced with the word \"pickled\". Foods that are pickled include vegetables, fruits, mushrooms, meats, fish, dairy and eggs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Gherkins_and_Onions.JPG/330px-Gherkins_and_Onions.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pickling","wikidata":"Q1503967"},{"title":"Bean","blocks":[{"text":"A bean is the seed of plants in many genera of the legume family (Fabaceae) used as a vegetable for human consumption or animal feed. The seeds are sold fresh or preserved through drying (a pulse). Beans have been cultivated since the seventh millennium BCE in Thailand, and since the second millennium BCE in Europe and in Peru. Most beans, with the exception of peas, are summer crops. As legumes, the plants fix nitrogen and form seeds with a high protein content. They are produced on a scale of millions of tons annually in many countries, with India as the largest producer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/French_beans_J1.JPG/330px-French_beans_J1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bean","wikidata":"Q379813"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg/500px-Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg/500px-Tursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Torshi, traditional pickles in Southeast Europe, West Asia and Northern Africa","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATursucu_Sahne_sokak_beyoglu_Istanbul_0644.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG/500px-PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG/1280px-PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG/500px-PikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kosher torshi in Israel","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APikiWiki_Israel_39626_Cities_in_Israel.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Indian-pickle.jpg/500px-Indian-pickle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 